WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.680
Agents that can execute code, write files, browse, query databases, call APIs, or run subprocesses need stronger boundaries than agents that only draft text.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.380
Sandboxing concepts help limit what agent-controlled operations can affect.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:29.840
The exact technology depends on the environment, but the stable principle is to reduce available authority: constrain files, network destinations, credentials, execution time, output size, storage, and side effects.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.460
File and workspace boundaries should be explicit.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.240
A tool that reads a project directory should not automatically read every file on the host.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.140
A tool that writes reports should not overwrite unrelated paths.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:49.600
Temporary workspaces, read-only modes, allowed directories, file type limits, and cleanup rules reduce accidental or unsafe effects.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:55.740
The agent should not receive broad filesystem access merely because one task needs a narrow file operation.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.480
Network and execution boundaries matter just as much.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:08.820
Destination allowlists, service-specific clients, request timeouts, response size limits, and rate controls help prevent uncontrolled outbound behavior.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:18.000
Subprocess, shell, code execution, browser automation, and database query tools should be treated as high-risk capabilities at a conceptual level.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:27.420
They need strong argument validation, isolation, observability, and review, especially when the input comes from natural language or retrieved content.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.280
Secret exposure is a common boundary failure.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:39.540
Environment variables, API keys, tokens, local credentials, and service metadata should not be casually available to agent-controlled tools.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:47.040
If a tool needs a credential, provide the narrow credential for that tool and operation, not a general runtime secret bundle.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:57.260
Good boundary controls make the safe path easy: least privilege, short timeouts, limited outputs, explicit destinations, and clear logs for what the agent attempted.
